Ah, now I get it. Matt for the multi-variate case ... sweet!

What surprised me was that for the Matt trick to work most effectivley, then the mu above should be included, i.e. I had this in my model:

    matrix[3,J] eta;

    eta <- diag_pre_multiply(sigma_eta, LR_eta * xi);

Now eta's were random effects with mean 0 and I wrote in my model

      kin  <- exp( (lkappa + lalpha_s)/2. + eta[2,j] );
      kout <- exp( (lkappa - lalpha_s)/2. + eta[3,j] );

and similar for eta[1,j]. This was already not so bad, but then I rewrote everything to

    vector[3] gamma;
    matrix[J,3] Eta;

    gamma[1] <- 0.;
    gamma[2] <- (lkappa + lalpha_s)/2.;
    gamma[3] <- (lkappa - lalpha_s)/2.;

    Eta <- transpose(rep_matrix(gamma, J) + diag_pre_multiply(sigma_eta, LR_eta * xi));

That is, I included as you did the overall mean. This gave me an even further boost in n_eff, albeit I don't really get why this is beneficial. Any idea?

... and of course, if you guys could provide some functions to make such optimization easier, many users will love you for that!

Anyway, I am very pleased with the performance of Stan now (I was all the time), but now Stan is much superior when it comes to multi-variate stuff at least on the problems I have seen.

Best,
Sebastian

On Monday, September 22, 2014 2:54:49 PM UTC+2, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Monday, September 22, 2014 7:55:55 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:
> > This should become part of the FAQ. Very important piece of information. If possible, then it would be really nice to have this trick done directly behind the scenes if applicable.
> >
> > May I ask why the "stochastic" representation is so much better when the LHS are parameters? Any theoretical insights here?
>
> I guess we could write an elliptical_transformation() function that would do the math slightly more quickly. But you would still have to reparameterize the model to have the right things in the parameters block, so it can't be fully automated.
>
> With NUTS, you always want the parameters to have a scale near 1 and be uncorrelated with other parameters. If you are using a
>
> beta[j] ~ multi_normal(mu, Sigma);
>
> hierarchical prior, then beta[j] depends on mu and even worse on Sigma. In particular, for a nearly singular proposal for Sigma, beta[j] has to be nearly linearly dependent (i.e. one of its elements is nearly fixed, given the other elements).
>
> But if you make beta[j] a transformed parameter with
>
> beta[j] <- mu + L * z[j];
>
> then all the elements of z are (a priori) unit-scaled, mutually independent, and independent of L. It really is the multivariate version of the Matt trick with a Cholesky factor of a covariance matrix serving the role of the standard deviation of a scalar. If L gets nearly-singular, then the last element of z[j] is not well-identified by the data but has a standard normal prior in the model block.
>
> Ben

Hi,

First of all thanks for Stan, it really seems well engineered and extremely useful for continuous data. That said I'm having a few problems with count data. I want to model counts as some sum over a true count and a noisy count (distributed as a Poisson with a relatively small rate). I've tried to set the model up as below but I'm running into a problem whereby Stan won't let me have integer parameters. Is Stan just not suitable for this sort of model? Perhaps there is some reparameterisation I haven't thought of that could help? The code belows samples data from the model in R and then tries to build a Stan model to fit it. I have a matrix of counts where each column has a scaling factor for both the rates of the noise and of the true counts. Each row has its own rate for the true counts. The observed counts are the sum of the noise and the true counts. I know it could be vectorized more efficiently but I need to get it working first.

N <-  10  # Number of rows
M <-   3  # Number of columns
col.sizes <- rlnorm(M, 0, 1)  # Scaling factor for each column

# Generate noise for each column
noise.lambda <- 1
noise <- matrix(0, nrow=N, ncol=M)
for(m in 1:M) {
    noise[,m] <- rpois(N, noise.lambda * col.sizes[m])
}

# Generate true counts
counts.lambda <- rlnorm(N, 2, 3)
counts <- matrix(0, nrow=N, ncol=M)
for(n in 1:N) {
    for(m in 1:M) {
        counts[n,m] <- rpois(1, counts.lambda[n] * col.sizes[m])
    }
}

# Observed counts are true counts + noise
obs.counts <- counts + noise
obs.counts

# Code as a Stan model
stan.code <- '
data {
    int<lower=0> N;  # Number of rows
    int<lower=0> M;  # Number of columns
    int<lower=0> obs[N,M];  # Observed counts
}
parameters {
    real<lower=0> colsizes[M];
    int<lower=0> noise[N,M];  # Noise
    int<lower=0> counts[N,M];  # True counts
    real<lower=0> countslambda[N];  # Rate for true counts
}
model {
    countslambda ~ lognormal(2, 3);
    for (n in 1:N) {
        for (m in 1:M) {
            noise[n,m] ~ poisson(colsizes[m]);
            counts[n,m] ~ poisson(countslambda[n] * colsizes[m]);
        }
    }
    # obs <- noise + counts;
}
'

# Fit the model
fit <- stan(model_code=stan.code)

Stan requires you to program the log probability function for
your model.  It only supports continuous parameters.  If you
have discrete parameters, they need to be marginalized out.

You're in luck with this model, because the Poisson is
well behaved:

  if x1 ~ Poisson(lambda1) and x2 ~ Poisson(lambda2),
  then x1 + x2 ~ Poisson(lambda1 + lambda2),

so you can replace this:

>     noise[n,m] ~ poisson(colsizes[m]);
>     counts[n,m] ~ poisson(countslambda[n] * colsizes[m]);
>     # obs <- noise + counts;

with

  obs ~ poisson(colsizes[m] + countslambda[n] * colsizes[m]);

and then you can get just eliminate the discrete
parameters noise[n,m] and counts[n,m].  I'd also be inclined
to rename the continuous parameters like countslambda and
colsizes with names that don't suggest their integer valued.
Though Andrew does like to name variables after their "standard"
name, so he might use lambda_counts for the mean parameter for
a discrete observed quantity counts.

I'm not sure exactly what you're trying to do, but given that
countslambda and colsizes are parameters constrained to be > 0,
you might consider dropping the constraint and instead exponentiating
the result --- that's the usual GLM form for Poisson regression.

- Bob

